    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. can you get more specific with the safety stock part though? i'm trying to figure out how to actually calculate a specific number for the safety stock value rather than just saying \"use a higher amount.\" like, what's the standard statistical way to account for that lead time uncertainty?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! To calculate a specific safety stock value that statistically accounts for lead time uncertainty, you can use the following formula:\n\n\nSafety Stock = Z-score * Standard Deviation of Lead Time Demand\n\n\nWhere the Z-score corresponds to the desired service level (a high Z-score for high service levels, and a low Z-score for lower service levels), and the Standard Deviation of Lead Time Demand (SLD) is the square root of the expected sales during lead time multiplied by the squared coefficient of variation of lead time.\n\n\nCalculating the expected sales during lead time (assuming constant demand), equals Average daily demand multiplied by the Lead Time. The coefficient of variation (CoV) for lead time is the standard deviation of lead time divided by the average lead time.\n\n\nTo complete the calculation, let's define each component:\n\n- Z-score: For a 95% service level, Z-score is approximately 1.65.\n- Average daily demand: Based on your historical data.\n- Lead time: The average time it takes for your suppliers to deliver. In the case of inconsistent lead times, use the average of the observed lead times.\n- Standard deviation of lead time demand: The standard deviation of sales during lead time multiplied by the squared CoV of lead time",
      "source": "target",
